#!/bin/bash
# Phase: 9x — the epilogue: the closing report
# Requires: P3 (~/.local/bin/drift); everything past that degrades rather than fails

# No `set -e`, alone among the scripts here. `drift` exits 1 on any finding (§7), and a
# finding is a true report rather than a failed run — the whole point of the epilogue is
# that it tells you what is wrong without pretending the run went wrong. chezmoi aborts on
# a non-zero script, so "the epilogue never fails the run" (§3) is a property of this
# file's exit status, which is why the last line is what it is.
set -uo pipefail

# `run_after_`, not `run_onchange_after_`: this script re-derives state on every run by
# design, so content-hash skipping would be precisely wrong for it — a converged machine
# would stop being told what it is still missing, which is the one thing only this script
# says (§4). `9x` is outside the phase range deliberately: the closing report is the run's
# epilogue, not a phase, and `80` would imply a P8 that does not exist.

# A heredoc rather than `printf`, here and at the tail: a format string that begins with a
# dash is read as an option, so the rule for a banner is simply not to hand it to `printf`.
cat <<'HEAD'

----------------------------------------------------------------------
The closing report
----------------------------------------------------------------------
HEAD

# Dynamic — what is missing *right now*.
#
# Each chezmoi script is a separate process, so failures cannot be accumulated in memory,
# and the obvious fix — a scratch file the phases append to — is exactly the recorded
# intermediate state the convergence invariant bans (§1). It has the same failure mode
# besides: after a re-run it goes stale, and then it lies. So nothing is replayed. The
# world is re-checked, which is correct on the first run and the fifth alike (§3).
#
# This is the same invocation a human types at a desk, with no extra flag: `drift` omits
# empty sections, so on a fresh machine the *Not in the repository* section simply is not
# printed and there is nothing a brief mode would have to suppress (§7).
#
# The managed `.zshrc` puts `~/.local/bin` first on PATH (§6.3), but this script runs in
# chezmoi's environment rather than a login shell — so the seeding 42 does is needed here
# for the same reason.
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"

printf '\n'
if command -v drift >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  # Its exit status is discarded rather than propagated: drift's 1 means "this machine
  # disagrees with the repository", which is the message, not a malfunction.
  drift || true
else
  printf 'drift is not on PATH, so nothing could be re-checked. Re-run the bootstrap.\n'
fi

# Static tail — what no script can perform and no script can verify.
#
# Everything above is derived from the machine. Everything below cannot be: it is GUI work
# behind accounts, and a self-attested "have you signed in? [y/N]" would be a prompt
# wearing a gate's clothes — it blocks the run while verifying nothing (§2). A manual step
# is either verifiable, in which case the gate refuses on it, or it is not, in which case
# it is a line here. There is no third category.
#
# Hand-written rather than derived from a declaration, and that is not rule 1 slipping:
# nothing in the repository declares these, because the repository does nothing about them
# (§6.3). A data file would be a first list, not a second one, with this script as its
# only reader.
cat <<'TAIL'

----------------------------------------------------------------------
The rest is yours — no script can do or check any of it
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Sign-ins other things depend on

  GitHub          Register this machine's public key under BOTH "SSH keys" and
                  "SSH signing keys" (P0 item 6). Authentication and signature
                  verification are separate registrations of the same key, and
                  the second one is the one that is silently forgotten.
  App Store       Sign in, then re-run the bootstrap if the report above is
                  short an App Store application: `mas` cannot install for an
                  account that is not signed in, and the phase is tolerant.
  claude.ai       Agent skills and plugins ride the account, not this
                  repository (ADR 0010). Sync is not instant — a newly enabled
                  marketplace has taken roughly thirteen minutes to land — so
                  do not read an empty ~/.claude/plugins/synced/ as a failure
                  for the first quarter of an hour.

Applications that restore themselves once you sign in

  Nothing here is managed and nothing here is broken — signing in is the whole
  restore, and this list exists so a rebuilt machine is not audited by memory.

  1Password, Chrome (its extensions arrive with Chrome sync), VS Code (Settings
  Sync carries settings, extensions and keybindings together), Raycast, Slack,
  ChatGPT, Mimestream, Clockify, WhatsApp, GCal, Docker Desktop.

Credential files, by path — the values live in 1Password

  These block a CLI and nothing else: no phase waits on them, which is why none
  of them is a gate item. Fetch one the first time its tool asks for it.

  ~/.ha-cli.env         `ha`   — homeassistant-cli
  ~/.cloudflare         `cli4` — cloudflare-cli4
  ~/.firefly_token      the Firefly III token
  ~/.ghostfolio_token   the Ghostfolio token

TAIL

# The last line, and the reason there is no `set -e` above: whatever was reported, the run
# ends having succeeded. The report is the deliverable; failing on it would make the one
# script that tells the truth about the machine the one that hides the rest of the run.
exit 0
